# Chromium in kiosk mode on the DSI panel, pointed at the forwarded port.
# A user unit, installed at ~/.config/systemd/user/. See README, "Developing
# against the panel".

[Unit]
Description=AI Terminal kiosk browser on the DSI panel
# Ordering only, and deliberately no `Wants=`. `Wants=` pulled the tunnel up
# whenever the kiosk started, whatever the tunnel's own enable state, and the
# kiosk is WantedBy=default.target — so every login started an `ssh -N -L
# 8000:127.0.0.1:8000`. That was right while the Mac was the only thing
# serving 8000. It stopped being right the moment ai-terminal.service shipped:
# the two bind the same address, the tunnel wins the race because uvicorn is
# still importing, and the panel then shows a laptop while looking perfectly
# healthy. It also undid `systemctl --user disable --now ai-terminal-tunnel`
# at the next kiosk start, which is the one command the shipped Pi depends on.
#
# `After=` stays. It is pure ordering and starts nothing, so a developer who
# wants the tunnel still enables or starts it by hand and still gets the kiosk
# sequenced behind it in the same transaction — which is the whole of what the
# old line bought them, minus the start nobody asked for.
#
# It was `Wants=` and not `Requires=` for a reason worth keeping written down:
# measured on the panel, under `Requires=`, `systemctl --user stop
# ai-terminal-tunnel` stopped the kiosk with it and starting the tunnel again
# left the screen dark — the propagation runs one way only. The kiosk has to
# outlive whatever is serving the port and let the page's own reconnect find
# it again, which is now also how it survives a server restart.
After=ai-terminal-tunnel.service

[Service]
# `Requires=` says the tunnel unit is running; it does not say the port is
# bound. `ssh -N` binds a moment after exec, and a Chromium that arrives in
# that moment gets a connection refused and an error page it never retries —
# a black panel that stays black until somebody restarts the unit by hand.
#
# Ask the server, not the socket. `ssh -N -L` binds this end whether or not
# anything answers on the far side, so in the ordinary daytime state — Mac
# awake, `live-server` down — a `nc -z` gate passes in under a second and
# Chromium gets a reset instead of a refusal, which is the same error page by
# another route. `/api/config` is cheap, has no side effects, and returns 200
# only once the app is importable and serving.
#
# `--max-time` because `curl` with no deadline is bounded only by ssh: the
# tunnel binds this end, so a Mac that stops answering mid-connection rather
# than refusing leaves one attempt hanging until `ServerAliveInterval=15` x
# `ServerAliveCountMax=3` gives up — 45 s of the window on a single iteration.
ExecStartPre=/bin/sh -c 'for _ in $(seq 60); do \
    /usr/bin/curl -fsS --max-time 5 -o /dev/null \
    http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/config && exit 0; sleep 2; done; exit 1'
# The loop above is a 120 s floor and systemd's start timeout is 90 s, so
# without this the gate is killed with a quarter of its attempts unmade and the
# unit fails with `a timeout was exceeded` — a message that reads like the
# server never came up, when what expired is the manager's patience with the
# gate. 150 leaves room for the loop plus one `--max-time` attempt overrunning
# the last sleep.
TimeoutStartSec=150
# WAYLAND_DISPLAY is already in the user manager's environment once labwc has
# imported it, but a unit started at boot can be ahead of that import. Set to
# the value the compositor uses here, which is also the one README's manual
# `grim` recipe exports.
Environment=WAYLAND_DISPLAY=wayland-0
# --autoplay-policy or the first spoken answer is silent until something on
# the page is tapped, which on a screen with no keyboard is the whole point.
# --password-store=basic keeps Chromium from waiting on a keyring nobody will
# unlock. The profile is its own, so a reset costs nothing.
#
# WebRtcPipeWireCamera off because the settings sheet lists microphones with
# `enumerateDevices` (app.js:1368-1370), and on Wayland that makes Chromium ask
# xdg-desktop-portal for the cameras too. Measured on the panel: a modal
# *Allow app to Use the Camera?* over the terminal on every launch of a fresh
# profile. There is no camera here — /dev/video19-21 are the Pi's own codec
# and ISP nodes — so the question has no right answer and nobody is standing
# there to give one.
#
# ?kiosk=1 is this page saying it is the one in the kitchen. The server cannot
# work it out — the panel reaches a development Mac down the tunnel in
# ai-terminal-tunnel.service, so its socket arrives from 127.0.0.1 exactly like
# a browser tab on that Mac — and what turns on it is where the wake word
# lands: without it a word said at this screen opens the microphone on
# whichever page connected last. It does not change the origin, so the grant in
# ai-terminal-chromium-policy.json still matches. See #132.
#
# --remote-debugging-port is not a profiling switch and it is not optional:
# the terminal drives this browser. #162 has the agent attach over CDP so it
# can put a page in front of whoever is standing at the panel, and so browser
# use runs on this profile — signed in — instead of a headless throwaway that
# is logged out of everything. A Chromium profile directory takes one process
# at a time, so there is no second browser to attach to instead; this is the
# only one. Load-bearing daily, so it is open at every boot rather than
# switched on when somebody needs it.
#
# What that costs, written here because the flag does not say it: the devtools
# protocol asks for no credential and is not a read-only view — whatever opens
# the socket can navigate this page, read it, run script in it, and fetch
# anything this browser can reach, carrying whatever sessions the profile
# holds. Loopback is the whole of the bound. With no
# --remote-debugging-address Chromium listens on 127.0.0.1 alone, so it is not
# on the LAN, and it must not be put there.
#
# It goes ahead of the URL because url_in() in tools/pi_deploy.py takes the one
# https?:// match off this line, and every reader of the kiosk's origin — the
# microphone grant included — goes through it.
# See README, "The kiosk's debugging port". #144.
ExecStart=/usr/bin/chromium --kiosk \
    --ozone-platform=wayland \
    --user-data-dir=%h/.cache/ai-terminal-kiosk \
    --password-store=basic \
    --autoplay-policy=no-user-gesture-required \
    --disable-features=WebRtcPipeWireCamera \
    --remote-debugging-port=9222 \
    --touch-events=enabled \
    http://localhost:8000/?kiosk=1
Restart=always
RestartSec=5

[Install]
WantedBy=default.target
